  <p class="answer-label">Answers</p>
  <h1 class="font-display">What to do during a binge urge</h1>

  <p class="answer-lead">
    A binge urge is like a wave: it rises, peaks, and falls — usually within 20 to 30 minutes.
    You do not have to defeat it. You just have to get to the other side, and there are things
    that make riding that window easier.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you're in it right now: this feeling has a ceiling. It doesn't climb forever, even though
    it feels like it will. Here's what people who live with binge urges say actually helps while
    the wave is up.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Change where you are.</strong> Not forever — just for now. A different room, the
    doorway, outside for a minute. The urge is loudest in the place where it started, and your
    feet are one of the few things that still listen to you when everything else is on autopilot.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Say it to someone — or something.</strong> An urge does a lot of its work in silence.
    Speaking it out loud — to a friend, a group chat, a companion like Kippi, even a note to
    yourself — breaks the isolation the urge needs. You don't have to say anything wise.
    "It's here" is enough.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Name what's underneath.</strong> A binge urge is usually not about hunger. Ask: what
    showed up right before it? Loneliness, anxiety, boredom, anger, a hard conversation? You don't
    have to fix that feeling right now. Naming it is enough to loosen the urge's grip a little.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Remember the clock.</strong> Rises, peaks, falls — usually 20 to 30 minutes if you
    don't act on it. You're not trying to win forever. You're trying to be somewhere else, with
    someone, for about the length of an episode of something.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Give your body one anchor.</strong> Feet flat on the floor. One slow breath out. Cold
    water on your hands. Not because it's magic — because it gives the minute a shape, and waves
    pass one minute at a time.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Delay instead of refusing.</strong> "Never again" is a fight you can't win at 11pm.
    "Not right now" is smaller and softer, and it works more often. You're not promising forever.
    You're promising ten minutes, and then deciding again.
  </p>

  <h2 class="font-display">Questions people ask in this moment</h2>

  <h3>How long does a binge urge last?</h3>
  <p>
    Usually 20 to 30 minutes, if you don't act on it. An urge behaves like a wave — it rises,
    peaks, and naturally falls. It feels endless from inside the rise; it isn't.
  </p>

  <h3>Can I stop a binge urge once it's started?</h3>
  <p>
    You can't always switch an urge off, and that's not a personal failure — it's how urges work.
    What you can change is what the window looks like: where you are, whether you're alone with
    it, and what you do with your hands and feet while it passes. Riding it out counts as much as
    never feeling it.
  </p>

  <h3>What if the urge wins anyway?</h3>
  <p>
    Then you're still in recovery. Relapse is a normal and expected part of recovery — not a
    failure. Nothing about you is broken and nothing resets. We wrote more about that here:
    <a href="/answers/relapse-is-recovery">Relapse is recovery</a>.
  </p>

